The feature that we describe here was inspired by
\titleref {The Algorithmic Beauty of Plants}
by Przemyslaw Prusinkiewicz and Aristid
Lindenmayer
(\url{https://archive.org/details/the-algorithmic-beauty-of-plants}}).
It started when Mikael wanted to draw a few Hilbert curves in order to
discuss and show the idea behind space-filling curves in his lectures.

It is well known that one can produce these kinds of graphics and other
fractal-like and tree-like nice images using so-called Lindenmayer systems, and
of course there are many tools available to do so. We did not know of any such
tool working in \METAPOST, though, and thought it would be fun to explore the techniques.

We started by very simple turtle methods to code macros that generated a path
recursively (that first approach will be shown below), then we got some wrappers,
but we quickly went to a (still quite simple) mix of \LUA\ and \METAPOST. We
could actually leave the path generation to \LUA\ completely, but then we don't
get the fun of playing and applying a feature that we added a few years ago:
turtle graphics.
